
  <sn><md>### Sources of Soil Pollution

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Enviromental%20Pollution%20and%20Mitigation/01.Introduction%20and%20Concepts/assets/Soil_Pollution.jpeg)

| **Category** | **Examples** |
|---|---|
| Industrial | Mining, smelting, factory discharges |
| Agricultural | Fertilizers, pesticides, contaminated water |
| Urban/Domestic | Landfills, e-waste, household chemicals |
| Transportation | Fuel leaks, vehicle emissions, road salts |
| Radioactive | Nuclear waste, accidents, fallout |
| Natural | Volcanic eruptions, rock weathering |


1. **Mining Activities**: Extraction and processing of minerals deposit tailings rich in heavy metals (arsenic, cadmium, lead) and acidic compounds onto surrounding soil, rendering it infertile.

2. **Sewage Sludge and Wastewater Irrigation**: Using untreated sewage sludge or contaminated wastewater for irrigation introduces heavy metals, pathogens, pharmaceuticals, and microplastics into the soil.

3. **Radioactive Sources**: Nuclear accidents, improper disposal of radioactive waste, and atmospheric fallout deposit radionuclides (cesium-137, strontium-90) into soil, causing long-term contamination.

4. **Oil and Gas Exploration**: Drilling activities, pipeline leaks, and produced water spills contaminate soil with petroleum hydrocarbons, brine, and heavy metals.

5. **Military Activities**: Explosives, chemical agents, and heavy metals from training grounds and munitions testing contaminate soil with toxic residues.

6. **Natural Sources**: Volcanic eruptions, weathering of rocks, and forest fires naturally release heavy metals (arsenic, selenium, mercury) into the soil.

7. **Atmospheric Deposition**: Air pollutants from industries and vehicles settle onto soil through rain, snow, and dust, adding to the pollutant load.

8. **Improper Hazardous Waste Disposal**: Illegal dumping of industrial solvents, acids, and toxic chemicals directly contaminates soil and groundwater.


